## §2 Execution phases - `POD_execute_combat_move`

Every combat move runs through one generic effect:
**`POD_execute_combat_move`** (in `pod_combat_move_effects.txt`). There
is no per-move code. The effect reads the move's attributes off its
`POD_combat_move` story_cycle (passed in as `$STORY$`) and applies
whatever it finds — damage, heals, stance flags, tier buffs, reactions,
summons. A move *is* the set of variables on its story; execution is the
engine that interprets them.

**Caller scope is the combat host** (the PC, who holds all combat
state). Before the call, the caller loads the workspace alias pair
(`pod_combat_actor_*` / `pod_combat_target_*`) onto the host for whoever
is acting this turn:

- **actor** = whoever is taking the action (the PC, or any AI combatant)
- **target** = the current single target (rotates per AoE iteration /
  per AI target-swap)

Two callers drive it: the `pod_combat_execute_move` scripted GUI (PC
button) and `pod_combat_ai_select_and_execute_move_effect` (AI turn).

### Phases

The body is a flat sequence of `if` blocks, each guarded on `$STORY$ = {
has_variable = ... }`. They run **top to bottom**; a move only triggers
the phases whose variables it carries. The ordering is load-bearing —
see **Phase ordering rules** below before moving anything.

| Phase | What it does |
|---|---|
| §0 | Save the host as `scope:combat_host`. Long-lived; AoE iteration bodies re-enter host scope through it without their own root-save. |
| §1 | Spend `ap_cost` AP (`pod_combat_spend_ap_effect`). |
| §2 | Reset the result axes (`pod_combat_clear_last_result_vars_effect`) and set `pod_combat_last_damage = 0`. Absence of a `pod_combat_last_*` var now means "no outcome yet" for this move. |
| §3 | Pay `energy_cost` from the actor's splat pool. Scope-switches to `var:pod_combat_actor_char` first (`POD_splat_agnostic_energy_cost` drains the *current* scope's pool — without the switch every AI cast would drain the PC). Also bumps the AI energy-attrition counter. |
| §4 | Pay CK3 resources (`gold_cost` / `stress_cost` / `piety_cost` / `prestige_cost`). **PC only** — gated on `pod_combat_current_actor_team = flag:player` AND position 0. AI turns are free. |
| §5 | Strip the single target's stances: `bypass_dodge` removes the dodge flag; `bypass_stealth` runs the workspace stealth-break (which also subtracts the +30 stealth hit bonus). Runs **before** §6 so damage resolves against the stripped state. |
| §5b | `team_enemy` only: broadcast tier debuffs and stance-strips to **all** enemy slots before the damage loop, so each per-target hit respects them (`pod_combat_apply_team_enemy_debuffs`). |
| §6 | **HP damage dispatch.** Routes `single_enemy` vs `team_enemy`. Resolves the hit and writes the result axes `pod_combat_last_outcome = flag:attack` + `pod_combat_last_attack_result` on the host. See **Damage pipeline**. |
| §7 | Morale damage (`pod_combat_deal_morale_damage_effect`, or the team variant). Own pipeline: auto-hit, no guard, no soak, but stealth/dodge still block. Rider-gated by `morale_damage_condition`. |
| §8 | Self-damage to the actor (`pod_combat_deal_hp_damage_bypass_guard_effect`, `REASON = duel` — superficial, soakable). Rider-gated by `self_damage_condition`. Lifts the post-soak amount into `pod_combat_last_self_damage`; the attack's result axes are untouched (recoil never overwrites the main outcome). |
| §9 | HP heal. `heal_type = aggravated` heals aggravated boxes too; else superficial (caps at `max_hp − agg_damage`). Routes to actor / single-ally / `team_ally`. Rider-gated by `heal_condition` (drain moves auto-get `on_hp_damage`). Must run **after** §6 so the rider reads the right outcome. |
| §10 | Morale heal (`pod_combat_restore_morale_effect`). Routes to actor / single-ally / `team_ally`. No rider condition. |
| §11 | Tier buffs (→ actor) and debuffs (→ target). Each write is **upgrade-only** (fires only on first application or a strictly higher tier). Soak tiers also bridge to the character as `pod_combat_tier_soak_*` vars so PoD's wound rolls pick them up. Recalc fires per axis when its tier var was present. See **Tier system**. |
| §12 | Non-tier stance flags on the actor: `set_guard`, `dodge`, `stealth` (idempotent), and `remove_guard` (strips the target's guard). |
| §12b | `team_ally` only: broadcast §11/§12's actor buffs (tiers + stance flags + soak bridge var) to all ally slots (`pod_combat_apply_team_ally_buffs`). |
| §13 | Arm retaliation stances on the actor for **next** turn's §15: `retaliate_if_hit` (counters on contact — thorns) / `retaliate_if_missed` (counters on evasion — riposte). Amount falls back `retaliate_damage → hp_damage → 1`. Seeds `_agg` and the counter kind (morale wins over a damage source). |
| §13b | Damage-over-time: seed `slot_dot_amount/rounds/kind/aggravated` on the target's slot (single-target). Kind is seeded as literal flags (default `duel`, per-source override, morale wins); re-apply refreshes. Pushes the per-type "is afflicted" log row. Ticks fire at round end — see **Combat log** / **Turn loop**. |
| §13c | Mind control (`mind_control` flag): mark the target slot `slot_controlled` (steals one turn). Players are immune; `POD_has_strong_mental_defenses_trigger` blocks unless the caster passes `can_bypass_mental_defenses_trigger`. Blocked casts post a resist row. Single-target only. |
| §14 | Parametric flags: `ap_gain` (adds to `pod_combat_current_ap`), `ap_drain` (take-max into the target mirror, consumed at the target's next turn start), `initiative_debuff` (negated into `pod_combat_target_init_bonus`, consumed at round end). |
| §14b | `summons_combatant`: seat a new combatant on the caster's team mid-fight (`pod_combat_summon_combatant_glue_effect`). After the damage phases (a same-cast AoE can't hit the new unit), before §15 (it can't counter on its summon turn); it acts next round. |
| §15 | **Reaction phase.** Fire any retaliation the target armed on a previous turn (`pod_combat_check_retaliation_effect`). Skipped for `team_enemy` AoE (a reaction can only fire on one workspace target). See **Reactions**. |
| §16 | Push the combat-log row (`pod_combat_push_log_effect`). AoE-aware gate, below. |

### The §16 log gate

Non-AoE moves always push their one summary row. AoE moves are
different: the AoE iterators in §5b/§6/§9/§10 push **one row per
target** as they go and set the `pod_combat_aoe_phase_pushed_rows`
tripwire. When that tripwire is set, §16 normally skips its wrap-up row
— the per-target rows already are the log entry.

§16 fires anyway when actor-side state landed that the per-target rows
could not carry. The gate is an `OR` that fires the push when **any** of
these hold:

- `pod_combat_aoe_phase_pushed_rows` is **not** set (non-AoE path —
  always push), or
- `pod_combat_last_heal_amount` is set (actor self-heal), or
- `pod_combat_last_morale_heal_amount` is set (actor self-morale-heal),
  or
- `pod_combat_last_retal_fired` is set (a counter resolved), or
- `pod_combat_last_self_damage` is set (recoil is applied once to the
  actor, not per AoE target, so the per-target rows never carry it).

### Phase ordering rules

These orderings are required, not stylistic:

- **§5 / §5b before §6.** Damage must resolve against stripped stances.
  Reversed, hits land on un-stripped dodge/stealth/guard.
- **§7 / §8 / §9 after §6.** Their rider gates read
  `pod_combat_last_outcome` and `pod_combat_last_attack_result`, both
  written by §6. Reordering breaks the `on_hp_damage` / `on_contact` /
  `on_miss` conditions.
- **§15 after §6.** Retaliation gates on §6's outcome; running it
  earlier reads stale state from the previous move.
- **§16 last.** The log row captures every prior axis and amount field.
  Pushed earlier, it records a partial result and misses the rider
  amounts later phases populate.
- **§14b (summon) after the damage/AoE phases and before §15.** Keeps a
  same-cast AoE off the new unit and stops it countering on its summon
  turn.

### Damage pipeline

HP damage is the one place combat hands work to PoD. The pipeline
decides **whether** the blow connects (hit roll, guard, dodge/stealth,
spirit gate), then routes the connecting blow into PoD's wound system,
which owns **how much** lands (soak, splat weakness, aggravated
classification, the death check). Combat never touches wound boxes
directly.

#### Capability tags decide the pipeline shape

Each move carries up to four capability flags on its story, set at
generation time. The contact resolver
(`pod_combat_resolve_contact_effect`) reads them off
`scope:current_move` and walks the same decision tree for every move,
branching only where a flag is present:

| Capability flag | Effect when present |
|---|---|
| `checks_spirit_gate` | A physical attack passes harmlessly through an incorporeal spirit target unless the actor has the spirit-damage capability. |
| `requires_hit_roll` | Roll `pod_combat_current_hit_chance` vs 100. Absent = auto-hit. |
| `checks_guard` | Consume the target's guard and block the blow. Absent = guard does nothing (this is how a move "bypasses guard"). |
| `checks_stance` | Run stance consumption: stealth then dodge auto-miss the blow. |

An `attack_type` of physical / supernatural / mental is shorthand for a
preset of these four flags, which a move overrides by setting flags
directly. There is no `var:attack_type` at
runtime — only the resolved flags. The three shapes:

| `attack_type` | spirit_gate | hit_roll | guard | stance | Character |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **physical** | yes | yes | yes | yes | Real blow: can miss, be guarded, be dodged, and whiffs on spirits. |
| **supernatural** | no | no | no | yes | Auto-hits, ignores guard, hits spirits; only dodge/stealth evade it. |
| **mental** | no | no | no | yes | Same evasion profile, but the payload is morale-only (no HP path). |

(A holy physical weapon vs spirits keeps `checks_spirit_gate` but the
actor carries the spirit-damage capability; a spirit-respecting low-tier
discipline is supernatural with `override_checks_spirit_gate = yes`; an
always-hits physical move is physical with `override_requires_hit_roll =
no`.)

#### Contact resolution

`pod_combat_resolve_contact_effect` walks the tree and writes
`scope:contact_result` (a resolver-local integer, distinct from the
result-axis flags):

1. **Spirit gate** — if the move checks it, the target is an incorporeal
   spirit, and the actor can't damage spirits → result `6`, logged as
   `missed`.
2. **Stance consume** — if `checks_stance`, run
   `pod_combat_consume_target_stance_effect`: stealth first (result `4`,
   logged `stealthed`), then dodge (result `3`, logged `dodged`).
   Stealth is consumed before dodge so a stealthed-and-dodging target
   keeps its dodge for the next attack.
3. **Hit roll** — if `requires_hit_roll`, a `random_list` weighted by
   `pod_combat_current_hit_chance` either misses (result `2`, logged
   `missed`) or proceeds. No flag = auto-hit, fall through.
4. **Guard** — `pod_combat_check_guard_effect`: if `checks_guard` and
   the target is guarding, consume the guard and block (result `1`,
   logged `guard_blocked`). Else result `0` (hit).

Every non-hit branch sets the result axes and `pod_combat_last_damage =
0`. Only result `0` reaches the payload
(`pod_combat_apply_damage_payload_effect`). Actor stealth is consumed
after resolution regardless of outcome — committing the action breaks
stealth even on a miss.

#### `dispatch_by_source` — damage source → PoD death reason

Before the payload runs, `pod_combat_dispatch_by_source` maps the move's
`damage_source` flag to a PoD `death_reason` suffix (`$REASON$`), which
PoD uses for splat-weakness multipliers and aggravated classification:

| `damage_source` | `death_reason` |
|---|---|
| (none) | `duel` |
| `fire` | `fire` |
| `cold` | `cold` |
| `lightning` | `lightning` |
| `sun` | `sun` |
| `silver` | `silver` |
| `gold` | `gold` |
| `holy` | `true_faith` |
| `balefire` | `balefire` |

The mapping is identity in seven of eight cases. **`holy` maps to
`true_faith`**, not `holy_damage`, because PoD keys aggravated promotion
against `true_faith`. The same map is mirrored in
`pod_combat_deal_typed_retal_damage_effect` for sourced counters. To add
a source: add a branch here plus the loc/icon in
`pod_combat_static_l_english.yml` and `PODCombatDamageSourceMarker`.

#### Soak and the wound write

The connecting blow goes through `pod_combat_route_to_pod_damage`, which
shims into PoD's wound pipeline (`POD_increase_wounds_hostile_effect`).
**Soak lives entirely in PoD** — combat passes raw `COUNT`; PoD applies
the soak roll, the splat-weakness multiplier, the damage cap, immunity,
and the inline death check.

The route helper returns scope values only (no host-var side effects):
`scope:damage_dealt` (post-soak amount; 0 if fully soaked or in practice
mode) and `scope:attack_result_flag` (`flag:hp_damage` / `soaked` /
`utility`). Because the wound SVs read 0 on a dead character, it
snapshots superficial and aggravated box counts **separately** before
the write and takes `max(0, sup_delta) + agg_delta` — a single total-box
delta would read 0 when PoD's aggravated push-out converts existing
superficial boxes to aggravated, falsely classifying a real hit as
soaked. Morale spillover scales off the actual post-soak damage
(`pod_combat_morale_from_hp_ratio`).

#### The spirit gate

The spirit gate is the one combat-only protection in the connect step
(PoD has no concept of it). A physical attack against an incorporeal
spirit target lands only if the actor carries the spirit-damage
capability; otherwise it no-ops and the row logs as a miss. The resolver
applies it for moves with `checks_spirit_gate`. Retaliation skips the
resolver, so `pod_combat_apply_retaliation_helper` hand-rolls the same
gate: a physical counter against an incorporeal spirit only lands with
the capability, else it passes through harmlessly and the main-attack
log line is preserved.

#### Aggravated routing

A move with `damage_type = aggravated` forces `REASON = duel_aggravated`
in `pod_combat_apply_single_hp_damage`, overriding whatever
`damage_source` would have mapped to. PoD then applies the blow as
aggravated. The same substitution drives the aggravated branches in
self-damage and untyped DoT ticks (`slot_dot_aggravated = 1`).

