## §8 Combat end

Combat ends when one team is wiped, the PC concedes, or the player
auto-resolves. All three paths converge: set `pod_combat_winner` +
`pod_combat_loser`, set `pod_combat_is_over`, then the GUI swaps in the
**End Combat** button which calls `pod_combat_end_combat_effect`.

### Victory check

`pod_combat_check_group_victory_effect` runs at every action boundary
(workspace save, end of each AI turn, after a DoT tick). It is not run
per damage event — an AoE wipe would otherwise emit one cleanup per kill
— so cleanup stays atomic. It checks three conditions in order, first
match wins:

| # | Condition | Result |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | No alive enemy slot | player wins; loser = enemy leader (enemy/0) |
| 2 | PC slot (player/0) KO'd | player loses; the PC can't act, so combat can't continue |
| 3 | No alive player-team slot | player loses (covers a bypassed PC death check) |

A match sets `pod_combat_winner`, `pod_combat_loser`,
`pod_combat_is_over`, saves the enemy leader as
`scope:pod_combat_target`, and pushes the victory/defeat log row. The
combat window stays open; the player clicks End Combat to dismiss.

### Concede and auto-resolve

Two extra outcome paths exist beside the win/loss check.

**Concede** is the combat-window X button (`pod_combat_close_window`)
while combat is in progress: it sets the PC as loser via
`pod_combat_set_outcome_pair_effect = { PC_WON = no }`, zeroes the
conceding leader's `slot_morale` (so the portrait reads defeated and the
leader takes full Mental Strain), sets `pod_combat_is_over`, and pushes
the defeat row. After combat is resolved the same X button instead
closes the window.

**Auto-resolve** (`pod_combat_auto_resolve_effect`) skips the rest of
the fight with a prowess-sum roll. It sums alive-slot `slot_prowess` per
team, adds per-team jitter (`sum + rand(0..sum)`), and the higher score
wins. `slot_prowess` is captured at init (after discipline buffs), so it
already reflects in-combat prowess. It writes the outcome via
`set_outcome_pair_effect` (`PC_WON = yes` when the player score
ties-or-beats the enemy), zeroes the loser leader's morale, and pushes
the matching log row.

Both paths write the winner/loser pair atomically through
`pod_combat_set_outcome_pair_effect`, which always sets **both** vars on
**both** branches — the post-combat consumer gates on both, so a
half-set pair would silently downgrade a decisive result to a draw. On a
PC win the loser is the enemy leader; on an enemy win the winner is
recorded by `pod_combat_save_enemy_victor_effect`, which records the
lowest-position alive enemy slot's char (falling back to the enemy
leader only when every enemy is KO'd), so a downed leader is never
recorded as the victor.

### Ending the combat

`pod_combat_end_combat_effect` is re-entry guarded on
`pod_combat_active` (the GUI execute and End Combat both call it). It:

1. **Non-fatal cap + strip** (`pod_combat_apply_nonfatal_effect`, only
   when `fatality = no`): caps each combatant's wound track to keep at
   least one empty box (peeling a superficial wound first, else an
   aggravated one), then strips the `pod_combat_nonfatal_active`
   death-gate flag — in that order, so the inline death check can't fire
   during the cap pass.
2. **Morale → Mental Strain** (`pod_combat_apply_morale_strain_effect`):
   one batched pass over alive combatants. Each takes
   `POD_static_stress_gain` from its final morale deficit —
   `(pod_combat_max_morale − slot_morale) ×
   pod_combat_morale_stress_multiplier` (multiplier = 5). Batched to one
   pass because `POD_static_stress_gain` is heavy (per-call skill
   recalc).
3. Saves winner/loser as named scopes and runs
   `pod_combat_duel_end_effect`, which strips each slot's discipline
   battle modifiers and the `pod_combat_duel_active` marker while slot
   vars are still intact.
4. Branches on whether this was a duel-driven combat. If
   `pod_combat_sc_context` exists, it removes `pod_combat_active`
   (keeping `pod_combat_is_over` as a re-entry guard) and fires
   `pod_combat.9999`. Otherwise (standalone/debug combat) it shows a
   victory/defeat toast and runs `pod_combat_cleanup_effect`
   immediately.

### Post-combat handoff

For duel-driven combat, `pod_combat.9999` restores the original duel
context and resolves the outcome in six phases:

1. Reconstruct `sc_initiator` / `sc_attacker` / `sc_defender`, the alias
   scopes, and `sc_victor` / `sc_loser` from the PC's
   `pod_combat_sc_context` pointer.
2. Rebuild the parent-event aliases (the whitelist stashed by the
   launcher).
3. Round-trip the rule flags (fatality / fixed / locale) into
   saved-scope flag values.
4. **Apply death by fatality mode.** Wounds were written live and PoD
   death-checked inline as they landed, so a lethal hit already killed
   the loser; this phase only forces `pod_death` for guaranteed-kill
   modes (`fatality = always`, and `default` on tribal governments) when
   the live damage fell short, each guarded by `is_alive` so it never
   re-kills a corpse. `practice` and `no` apply no death; `possible`
   trusts the live damage.
5. Fire the follow-up event (`OUTPUT_EVENT`), with an initiator →
   defender → attacker fallback chain matching vanilla.
6. **Cleanup** (`pod_combat_cleanup_effect`): clears combat tiers,
   removes the host combat vars and workspace mirrors, `end_story`s the
   log entries and the context story, then runs
   `pod_combat_group_cleanup_effect` (drops pending reinforcements,
   clears AI scratch, despawns summons, tears down slot stories, clears
   group state) and removes `pod_combat_step_mode`. The context story's
   atomic `end_story` drops all `sc_*` refs, rule flags, alias
   snapshots, and the follow-up id at once.

